# Files that must arrive byte-identical to the blob they were committed from.

# The bytes under crates/bv-export/assets are embedded with `include_bytes!`
# and asserted against the Go oracle by exact size and sha256. A fresh Git for
# Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so a checkout rewrites LF to CRLF
# in anything it considers text, and every embedded asset came back a few bytes
# longer than the blob:
#
#     assertion `left == right` failed: vendor/alpine-collapse.min.js length
#     assertion `left == right` failed: viewer.js size
#     assertion `left == right` failed: index.html size
#     assertion failed: html.starts_with("<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">")
#
# None of those files had changed. `-text` turns off line-ending conversion in
# both directions, which is what `include_bytes!` needs — and unlike a
# `core.autocrlf=false` step in the workflow, it holds for every checkout
# regardless of how the machine is configured.
crates/bv-export/assets/** -text

# The golden corpus is byte-parity data as well. The JSON cases tolerate CRLF
# because the gate parses them, but the TOON corpus is compared as text and the
# whole point of the corpus is that it is exactly what the oracle wrote.
golden/** -text
